<Hub pillarId="using-assistants">
  <p>Use the tool. Keep your judgment. A fluent reply is a draft with good posture, not a source.</p>
  <h2>What is the assistant doing?</h2>
  <p>
    A ChatGPT-style assistant is a general-purpose system with a chat window. The <a href="https://internationalaisafetyreport.org/sites/default/files/2026-02/international-ai-safety-report-2026.pdf">International AI Safety Report 2026</a> says current systems sometimes fabricate information, produce flawed code, and give misleading advice. Techniques can lower how often that happens. The report says they do not yet reach the level many high-stakes settings need.
  </p>
  <h2>What should you do with the reply?</h2>
  <p>
    Treat it as a draft. <a href="https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.AI.600-1">NIST</a> says confabulations are a natural result of how generative models are designed, and that users may be misled because the false content sounds confident. If the answer matters, open a primary source and check the claim there.
  </p>
  <p>
    The report also names automation bias: the tendency to trust an automated output without enough scrutiny. The habit that fights it is small. Read the reply, then read something that is not the reply.
  </p>
  <p>The step-by-step page is <a href="/articles/how-to-use-an-ai-assistant/">How to use an AI assistant safely</a>.</p>
</Hub>
